      ],
      "id": "0kr52rr"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "bsemu1e",
      "content": "Fire safety refers to the measures taken to prevent the outbreak of fire and to control it if it occurs. In workshops, factories, and industrial areas, fire hazards are common due to the presence of fuels, electrical systems, welding sparks, and chemicals. Proper fire safety practices protect workers, machines, and property from damage."
    },
    {
      "id": "82ta5jf",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "2. What is Fire?"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "5o4un3v",
      "content": "Fire is a chemical reaction known as combustion. It occurs when a combustible material reacts rapidly with oxygen in the presence of heat, producing light, heat, and flames."
    },
    {
      "id": "zuyr0yk",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "3. Fire Triangle"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "drlmdjk",
      "content": "The Fire Triangle explains the three essential elements required for a fire to occur."
    },
    {
      "id": "sl658gj",
      "type": "table",
      "rows": [
        {
          "isHeader": true,
          "cells": [
            "<p><strong>Element</strong></p>",
            "<p><strong>Explanation</strong></p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Heat</p>",
            "<p>Source of ignition such as sparks, flames, or hot surfaces</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Fuel</p>",
            "<p>Combustible material like wood, paper, petrol, or oil</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Oxygen</p>",
            "<p>Supports the combustion process and allows fire to continue burning</p>"
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "wdtxsew",
      "content": "If any one of these three elements is removed, the fire will stop. This principle is used in firefighting."
    },
    {
      "id": "24vqemw",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "4. Common Causes of Fire in Workshops"
    },
    {
      "id": "sqcorrr",
      "type": "list",
      "items": [
        "Electrical short circuits",
        "Overloaded electrical equipment",
        "Flammable liquids and gases",
        "Welding and cutting sparks",
        "Smoking in restricted areas",
        "Poor storage of chemicals"
      ]
    },
    {
